Intervention do(A=a) replaces equation for A. Counterfactual probability P(O_j | do(A)) or P(O_{A=a}=o). Expected causal utility U(a)=Σ_o P(O_{a}=o)D(o). For uncertainty over causal structure: mixture over causal models/hypotheses: U(a)=Σ_h P(h) E_h[u|do_h(a)] (if dependency hypotheses). Need mention this is my addition/standard development, not explicit source. It addresses source's open question but depends on causal model. If no model, CDT underdetermined. Imaging can be seen as closest-world semantics; do-calculus gives formal if causal assumptions specified.\n- Policies: For policy vs actions. In sequential decisions, evaluate policy π by intervention setting each decision node to π(inputs). But CDT may evaluate each local choice holding fixed future policy? Need discuss. Source not detailed. Need define: A policy is function from information states to actions. Causal expected utility of policy π is E[u | do(π)], where do(π) sets decision variables according to π. This allows dependencies through observations, but only causal. If evaluating alternative actions at a single choice node, can compare U(a) with background policy fixed. However in dynamic problems, local greedy CDT can fail if it doesn't commit? Need mention source's criticism of diachronic Dutch books maybe. Need careful: CDT as a theory of rational choice should maximize expected utility of policies under intervention. If only evaluates acts one at a time, might be dynamically inconsistent. My operational proposal: evaluate policies globally when available. Distinguish from source? Source says CDT evaluates actions, not explicit policies. Need state extension.\n- Counterfactuals: dependency hypotheses. Gibbard and Harper axiom note independence of A and A->O_j yields equality with conditional probability. Need explain: If action statistically independent of counterfactuals, then causal and evidential coincide. In many ordinary cases, actions independent of states, so CDT=EDT. When not independent (common causes, predictors), diverge.\n- Mathematical details:\n  Let A finite actions, O outcomes. EDT: V_EDT(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | A=a) D(o). CDT: V_CDT(a)=Σ_o P(a □→ O=o) D(o). Use Lewis notation maybe P(A □→ O). If using SCM: V_CDT(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | do(A=a))D(o). If outcomes utilities depend on action directly? Use utility function U(a,s): V_CDT(a)=Σ_s P(S=s | do(a))U(a,s). Or D(O_j) after outcome includes action? Need mention.\n  In possible-worlds: P(a □→ o)=Σ_w P(w) 1[w_a ∈ O_o] where w_a is closest a-world. Imaging: shift mass from w to closest a-worlds. Need formula: P_im(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) P_A(w)(O) maybe.\n  Independence condition: If A independent of counterfactual A□→O_j, then P(A□→O_j)=P(A□→O_j | A)=? Need source says statistical independence of A and A→O_j suffices to guarantee P(A→O_j)=P(O_j|A) under axioms. We can state without proof.\n- Examples with calculations:\n  Apple/orange simple. Apple good/bad: action eat apple; state good/bad; outcomes happy/sad. CDT expected = P(good)*D(happy)+P(bad)*D(sad), assuming eating causes happiness/sadness depending state. Compare orange.\n  David: maybe table. Let C=charismatic, action S=summon, outcome R=revolt, B=Bathsheba pleasure. P(C)=p. Utility: summon gives pleasure b if no revolt, cost maybe. Revolt cost -r. CDT: R not caused by S, only by C, so U(S)=P(C)(b)+P(not C)(b?) maybe no revolt effect? Actually if uncharismatic frequent revolt but not caused by unjust acts; maybe revolt happens regardless? If sending doesn't affect revolt, then U(S)=b - P(revolt|do(S))*r. P(revolt|do(S))=P(revolt) maybe. If summon has direct benefit, choose if benefit exceeds baseline revolt risk. EDT would use P(revolt|S) high because S evidence uncharismatic. Need not exact.\n  Newcomb: dominance.\n- Need word count 2400-3000. Need likely around 2600. Let's estimate. We can write with headings. Need references short. Could cite source Wikipedia and primary works. Since source is Wikipedia, need mention adapted from Wikipedia? Maybe references list: Gibbard & Harper 1981; Lewis 1981; Pearl 2009 (own addition); Joyce 2012; Egan 2007; Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021; Wikipedia source. Need avoid lengthy bibliography. But target includes references. Maybe 6-8 references.\n\nNeed ensure no false claims? We can mention Pearl's do-calculus as my operational proposal not source. Need distinguish source claims: CDT states choose action causing best expected outcome; formal by Gibbard Harper; difference from EDT; Lewis proof; independence axioms; examples; criticisms. Own: SCM formalization, policy extension, local vs global, using do operator, averaging over dependency hypotheses.\n\nNeed be precise enough for reader to decide unfamiliar cases. Need propose procedure:\n1. Specify action set and outcomes/utility.\n2. Specify causal model or dependency hypotheses: variables, arrows, exogenous uncertainty. Identify which variables are affected by action.\n3. For each action a, compute post-intervention distribution P(o | do(a)) by replacing action equation / selecting closest a-worlds. If multiple causal hypotheses h with credence q_h, compute mixture.\n4. Compute expected utility U_CDT(a).\n5. Choose argmax. For policies, specify policy and intervene on all decision nodes, compute global expected utility.\nNeed mention if causal model unavailable, CDT cannot yield unique answer; maybe EDT or other? Source says vagueness. Need propose if only statistical data and no causal assumptions, no purely statistical method can identify counterfactuals except under independence. Need caution.\n\nNeed explain relevant dependencies and counterfactuals:\n- Causal dependencies: action -> outcome paths; mediating variables; confounders. Holding fixed variables not on causal path? In interventions, variables upstream of action remain as prior; downstream affected. Noncausal dependencies (common causes, predictors) are not used for evaluating action except insofar as they inform prior over causal structure or exogenous states that action cannot change. However they may matter for predicting what you will do? For decision, if action is evidence of type, CDT holds type fixed under intervention. But if type affects utility directly, its prior enters. Need example: If you learn action tendency is evidence of disease, CDT doesn't choose action to make disease less likely unless action causally affects disease.\n- Counterfactuals: probability of \"if I were to do A, O would occur\". Not same as conditional \"given that I do A\". Need explain Lewis triviality result: cannot generally equate.\n- Imaging: possible worlds. If worlds W with prior P. For each world w, define closest A-world f_A(w). Then P(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) 1[f_A(w)⊨O] or if probabilistic tie, average. This captures moving probability mass. But source notes regress because need beliefs under certainty of A. In SCM, intervention avoids regress by structural equations.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"causal expected utility\" vs \"news value\". EDT asks: what news would the action give? CDT asks: what would the action bring about? Good phrase.\n\nNeed handle policies and commitment. In Newcomb, if agent could precommit to one-box before predictor, CDT would recommend precommitting if predictor responds to precommitment? Actually if predictor predicts precommitment, maybe. But at moment, two-box. This illustrates dynamic issues. Source mentions diachronic Dutch books. Need mention CDT may be exploited if predictor anticipates CDT's choices. My operational policy evaluation: If precommitment is an action now that causally influences later prediction, CDT can choose it. But if predictor predicts your algorithm, may cause problems. Need not overdo.\n\nPotential structure:\nTitle\n1. Introduction: two kinds of expected utility\n2. Basic formalism\n3. Counterfactual probabilities and interventions\n4. Comparing CDT and EDT; independence condition\n5. Examples: apple, David, Newcomb, maybe psychopath/death/adversarial\n6. Policies and sequential decisions\n7. Open problems and operational interpretation\n8. Conclusion\nReferences\n\nNeed word count. Let's draft about 2700 words.\n\nNeed be careful: user requested \"Researcher — mathematical essay / blog post explaining CDT and how to apply it to decision problems.\" They supplied human-written reference. Need not mention source as instructions. Need cite source? Could say \"This essay draws on the Wikipedia article 'Causal decision theory' (revision 1381555528).\" In references maybe. Need distinguish claims: maybe footnote-like parentheses \"The Wikipedia source reports...\" But essay can say \"The reference article states...\". Need maybe avoid over-quoting. Need include source claims vs own: use phrases: \"The source gives...\", \"I will add...\", \"This operationalization is not in the source but...\".\n\nNeed include mathematical notation robustly. Use LaTeX-ish.\n\nDetailed content:\n\nOpening:\n\"When an agent chooses, she can ask two questions: (1) If I learned I would take action a, what would I expect? (2) If I made myself take a, what would happen? EDT answers first; CDT answers second. CDT says choose action with highest expected causal consequence.\"\n\nDefine finite decision problem. Let A actions, O outcomes, D desirability. EDT:\nV_E(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | A=a)D(o). Conditional probability can reflect evidence.\nCDT source: Gibbard Harper:\nU(A)=Σ_j P(A → O_j)D(O_j). Use counterfactual arrow. Note source uses \"A necessarily implies O_j\" but better \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain\" probability.\nNeed mention if outcomes exhaustive mutually exclusive.\n\nThen interventions:\nI propose denote U_CDT(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | do(A=a))D(o). If uncertainty over states S: U(a)=Σ_s P(S=s | do(a))u(a,s). Under a causal Bayesian network/SCM, do(a) deletes incoming arrows to A and sets A=a; post-intervention distribution computed by truncated factorization. If model has variables X, joint P(x)=∏_i P(x_i|pa_i). After do(a): P_a(x)=∏_{i:X_i≠A}P(x_i|pa_i) * 1{a consistent}. This is precise. This is my addition; source only mentions counterfactual probabilities and imaging. Need state.\n\nHow to decide unfamiliar case: steps. Maybe include in section.\n\nIndependence:\nIf P(A□→O | A)=P(A□→O) and conditionals link to outcomes, then CDT=EDT. Source: Gibbard/Harper axioms, independence suffices. In ordinary decisions, action often independent of states, so both agree. Differences arise when action is evidence of state due to common cause or prediction.\n\nExamples:\nApple: states good/bad, utility. If P(good)=0.7, D(happy)=10, D(sad)=-5, apple U=0.7*10+0.3*(-5)=5.5; orange maybe certain 3; choose apple. Need mention if orange causes sad? Eh.\n\nDavid:\nDefine C charismatic. C fixed. Revolt R caused by C, not by summoning S. Pleasure b from S. CDT: P(R|do(S))=P(R). U(S)=b - rP(R). U(not)=0 - rP(R)? If no pleasure maybe 0. Since b>0, send. EDT: P(R|S)>P(R) because S evidence uncharismatic, so may abstain. Source says EDT abstains, CDT sends. Need mention if S could cause revolt, CDT would include that.\n\nNewcomb:\nActions T (two) and O (one). Box B content determined by predictor P before. If B contains M, two yields M+1000, one M; if empty, two 1000, one 0. For every state of B, two yields 1000 more: dominance. Thus CDT two-boxes, assuming no causal route from current choice to past prediction. EDT one-boxes because A=one has high conditional probability of M. Need mention predictor accuracy doesn't matter for dominance if perfect? With reliable but not perfect? If B already set, two still dominates: two gives +1000 relative to one in both contents. So CDT chooses two.\n\nCounterexamples/criticisms:\nSource says CDT vague due to no algorithm; imaging regress. Also alleged counterexamples: Death in Damascus, adversarial offer, psychopath button. Need explain one or two. Maybe psychopath: action press is evidence Paul psychopath; if psychopath pressing kills him? CDT holds psychopathy fixed and sees pressing causes psychopaths dead? Let's articulate carefully. The example: button kills all psychopaths; Paul confident only a psychopath would press; strongly prefers living with psychopaths to dying. Intuitively don't press. CDT, according to Egan, presses because if Paul is not a psychopath, pressing causes good (no psychopaths); if he is, maybe pressing causes death? But if he is quite confident only psychopath would press, his posterior after considering pressing? CDT uses prior? Hmm. Need present as source: \"Egan argues CDT endorses pressing because it evaluates the causal effect of pressing while holding fixed Paul's psychopath status, and the bad news that pressing provides about that status is ignored.\" That is enough. But if CDT includes probability he is psychopath and death utility very negative, might not press if prior high? The point is if prior low but action strong evidence, CDT still presses. We can state \"under certain priors\".\n\nDeath in Damascus: You choose stay/flee; Death predicted location. CDT sees no causal effect of choice on Death's prior prediction, so may randomize or choose based on priors; but whichever it chooses, if predictor reliable it expects Death there; instability. Source says CDT unable to process correlation and may make irrational decisions.\n\nAdversarial offer: Need explain maybe as money pump. Two boxes; can buy one or none. Seller puts $3 in boxes predicted not bought. CDT calculates average box has >$1.50 and buys, but seller profits. Need maybe skip or include brief. Since source includes, mention as recent variants. Need not fully compute.\n\nOperational interpretation:\nSource leaves algorithm open. Need develop:\n- Causal Bayesian network/SCM. Need say reasonable if agent can specify causal graph and parameters; otherwise CDT is underdetermined.\n- For counterfactuals in deterministic SCM with exogenous distribution: For world u, counterfactual outcome O_{a}(u) computed by solving equations with A=a. P(O_a=o)=P({u:O_a(u)=o}). This is crisp.\n- If only have