ed references at end maybe 200 words. Total within. Could use footnotes? No. Need no appendices.\n\nLet's start writing final. Need maybe include \"Source and own additions\" explicit. Use phrases:\n\"The reference article makes the following core claim...\"\n\"The operational interpretation below is mine (though standard in causal inference).\"\n\nNeed maybe mention Wikipedia source oldid. In references:\nWikipedia contributors, \"Causal decision theory,\" revision 1381555528 (2026). etc.\nGibbard and Harper 1981; Lewis 1981; Pearl 2009; Egan 2007; Joyce 2012; Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021. Need not too many.\n\nNeed ensure mathematical definitions precise. Let's formulate.\n\nPossible final:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory as Expected Utility under Intervention\n\nIntro.\n\n## 1. Two expected utilities\nLet A etc. EDT formula. CDT formula. Explanation.\n\n## 2. Counterfactual probabilities and interventions\nSource's arrow. Lewis not equal. Formal operationalization. SCM formula.\n\n## 3. Dependencies\nIndependence. Common causes.\n\n## 4. Examples\nApple, David, Newcomb.\n\n## 5. Policies\nPolicies extension.\n\n## 6. Criticism and open questions\nVagueness, imaging regress, counterexamples. Operational cautions.\n\nConclusion.\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed maybe include exact source quote? Not necessary.\n\nLet's be careful with math notation in plain text. Use \\(...\\). Need ensure formulas render. Use arrow \\(A \\mathbin{\\square\\!\\to} O\\) maybe. Source uses \\(A \\to O_j\\). Could define: I write \\(a \\counterfactual o\\) or \\(A \\boxright O\\). Simpler: use \\(P(A \\Rightarrow O_j)\\) for counterfactual? Source uses \\(A \\to O_j\\). But arrow could confuse with conditional. Need say: \"I will write \\(A \\mathrel{\\square\\to} O_j\\) for the counterfactual, reserving \\(|\\) for conditioning.\" Good.\n\nFormula: \\(U_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\sum_j P(a \\mathrel{\\square\\to} O_j)D(O_j)\\). Then operational \\(=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid do(A=a))D(O_j)\\). Need note equality under proposed interpretation.\n\nSCM factorization:\nObserved variables \\(X=(X_1,...,X_n)\\). Joint under model \\(M\\): \\(P(x)=\\prod_i P(x_i\\mid pa_i)\\). Intervention \\(do(A=a)\\): \\(P_a(x)=\\mathbf{1}_{x_A=a}\\prod_{i:X_i\\neq A}P(x_i\\mid pa_i)\\). If A has parents, ignore their prior effect because intervention overrides. Then expected utility.\n\nCounterfactual in SCM with exogenous U:\n\\(P(O_{a}=o)=P_U(\\{u: M_{A=a}(u)\\models O=o\\})\\). This is more precise.\n\nNeed discuss policies:\nSequential model with decision variables \\(D_1,...,D_k\\). Policy \\(\\pi=(\\pi_1,...,\\pi_k)\\), where \\(\\pi_i\\) maps observed parents to action. Intervention \\(do(\\pi)\\) sets each \\(D_i=\\pi_i(Pa_i)\\). Expected utility \\(U(\\pi)=E[U\\mid do(\\pi)]\\). Compare policies. If only single action, policy is trivial.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"holding fixed dependency hypotheses\" and Egan. Formula: \\(U(a)=\\sum_h p(h)U_h(a)\\) with p(h) unconditional, not conditional on a. This is source/Egan: CDT holds fixed unconditional credences. Need explain critics say irrational; defenders say can incorporate self-knowledge by including dispositions as variables or by choosing policies.\n\nNeed perhaps include \"ratifiability\"? Source mentions Harper ratifiability but not detail. Could mention briefly: some CDT responses require chosen action to be stable conditional on performing it, but source only citations. Maybe avoid.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Lewis proved probability of conditional does not equal conditional probability\". Need explain triviality result: If probability conditional equals conditional probability for all, trivial. We can mention no deep details.\n\nNeed maybe include \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" not necessary but source mentions. Could say Gibbard/Harper show under extra logical axioms independence yields equality; these axioms are controversial. Need include.\n\nLet's think about exact wording for David example. Need avoid confusion: If summoning is unjust, and unjust acts do not cause revolts, but uncharismatic kings prone to revolts due to bearing. Then CDT: The act of summoning does not change probability of revolt, except maybe if it changes people's beliefs? But in story, not. Thus expected causal utility includes pleasure. EDT: Since uncharismatic more likely to summon, summoning is evidence of uncharismatic and hence revolt; abstain. Good.\n\nNewcomb calculation: Let content C∈{M,0}. Utility two: C+1000; one: C. Since for both C, two > one, CDT chooses two regardless of P(C|do(two)). This is crisp. But if predictor can predict with time travel? If action causally affects past, CDT might one-box. Source says at moment predictor already predicted, action won't affect outcome. Good.\n\nAdversarial offer details: Let's ensure not wrong. Source: \"Two boxes on offer. Buyer may purchase one or none but not both. Each costs $1. Yesterday, seller put $3 in each box she predicted buyer not to acquire. Both believe prediction accurate with probability 0.75.\" CDT reasons at least one box contains $3. Therefore average box contains at least $1.50 causal EV > cost. Hence CDT requires buying one. However profitable for seller. Need maybe say \"the example is intended to show CDT can be exploited by a predictor who knows CDT's reasoning.\" But if buyer chooses which box? Symmetric. If no box? zero. CDT buys and loses expected? If cost $1, expected value >$1? But seller profit means buyer expected negative? Wait source says CDT requires buying one; however profitable for seller. If average contains at least $1.50, cost $1, buyer expected profit at least $0.50. How seller profit? Maybe seller receives $1 and pays expected $? Hmm maybe seller profit if CDT's reasoning false due to selection? Let's not dwell; say \"according to Oesterheld and Conitzer, such setups make CDT voluntarily lose money relative to available alternatives\". But if source says average at least 1.50, not lose? Maybe because if buyer buys one, the box likely empty? Let's parse more. Seller predicted buyer not to acquire. If buyer buys exactly one, one box not acquired. If predictions accurate 0.75 for each? At least one $3? CDT says average box $1.5. But actual expected from buying? If seller predicts CDT will buy a particular box? The seller can put money in the other? The source summary may be incomplete. Better not overly analyze. Mention as alleged counterexample without full math.\n\nDeath in Damascus: Need maybe provide decision table? You can stay in Damascus or flee to Aleppo. Death predicts where you'll be and goes there. If your choice does not causally affect Death's prior prediction, CDT might see no causal advantage to fleeing; but if Death reliable, whatever you choose is evidence he will be there. CDT may be unstable. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"Causal decision theory does not itself specify algorithm\". We can include as uncertainty. Then propose operational interpretation with causal models. Need justify: It captures counterfactual \"if A were done\" by intervention; handles dependencies; widely used in statistics/AI; resolves regress by separating beliefs about causal mechanisms from conditional beliefs about actions. But it imports causal assumptions; if source's philosophical counterfactuals differ, not guaranteed. Need state.\n\nNeed perhaps include \"when to use EDT instead\"? Not necessary. But mention if action and outcomes are only evidentially connected, CDT ignores; if you can affect the evidence (e.g. choose test to learn state) then causal.\n\nPotential issue: The source says Gibbard Harper formula: P(A -> O_j) is probability of it being case that A necessarily implies O_j. \"necessarily implies\" weird. We can interpret as counterfactual probability. Need maybe note \"the word 'necessarily' should not be read as logical necessity in all possible worlds; it is the truth of the counterfactual conditional in the agent's epistemic state.\" Hmm maybe.\n\nNeed ensure no unsupported claim \"most philosophers prefer CDT\" source says when required choose between CDT and EDT philosophers usually prefer CDT. Could mention. But not central.\n\nNeed maybe include \"policy vs action\" more. If evaluating policy, relevant dependencies include future observations caused by earlier actions. CDT with do(π) includes those. But if policy is a disposition that predictors predict, then CDT may need evaluate becoming that disposition? E.g. Newcomb: if before predictor you can cause yourself to be one-boxer, CDT may recommend making yourself one-box because predictor will observe disposition and fill box. At choice moment, two-box. This dynamic inconsistency is a criticism. Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"update on action\"? Joyce's defense: CDT can incorporate evidence about what one is likely to do by treating one's decision procedure or pre-action inclinations as evidence, but when the choice is made, causal calculation still ignores merely evidential links. Source says Joyce argues CDT naturally capable of taking into account information about what one is inclined/likely to do as evidence. We can state.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Dutch books\" Oesterheld/Conitzer: CDT agents can be Dutch booked if predictor knows CDT. Need mention in criticisms.\n\nWord count management. Let's draft about 2500. Need final not too short. I'll produce around 2700. Use headings. Need references maybe 150 words.\n\nLet's draft in final. But before final, let's roughly count. I can estimate 2500 words. Need maybe include equations and references. Let's write.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by What Actions Bring About\n\nParagraphs:\n1. Intro ~200\n2. Formal basics ~500\n3. Counterfactuals/intervention ~600\n4. Independence and EDT divergence ~250\n5. Examples: apple ~150, David ~250, Newcomb ~250\n6. Policies ~300\n7. Criticism/open questions ~450\n8. Conclusion ~100\nReferences ~150\nTotal ~3200? Need trim. Aim 2600. Maybe combine.\n\nLet's actually compose with concise but substantive.\n\nNeed ensure \"approximately 2,400–3,000 words\". Could be 2800. Good.\n\nLet's write final in polished essay.\n\nNeed include \"Source claims vs own\" explicit. Maybe after formula: \"The counterfactual formula is from Gibbard and Harper as reported in the reference. The interventionist reading using do(...) is an additional operational proposal, not explicit in the source.\" Good.\n\nNeed include mathematical procedure for unfamiliar cases. Could have boxed \"A practical recipe\". Include numbered list. That adds clarity and words. Good.\n\nPotential final recipe:\n1. Enumerate actions and outcomes; assign desirabilities.\n2. Build causal model or dependency hypotheses. Identify action node(s).\n3. For each action/policy, compute post-intervention distribution. If multiple hypotheses, average with unconditional credences.\n4. Sum utilities; choose max.\n5. Check causal assumptions; if only correlations, no unique CDT answer.\n\nNeed mention if action impossible? Source says assuming A possible. If impossible, undefined or exclude.\n\nNeed mention \"closest world\" formula:\nLet W possible worlds with credence P(w). Let f_a(w) be selected closest a-world. Then \\(P(a \\square\\to O)=\\sum_w P(w) 1[f_a(w)\\in O]\\). If ties, distribution. This is Lewis imaging. Source says regress. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"conditional excluded middle\" in independence section: Gibbard and Harper require two axioms, one related to conditional excluded middle, controversial. Need mention source. Could write: \"They showed that, given certain logical axioms (one connected to the principle of conditional excluded middle), statistical independence of A and the counterfactual A□→O_j suffices...\" Good.\n\nNeed perhaps mention Lewis triviality result footnote: \"if probability of conditional equals conditional probability generally, probability functions become trivial.\" Good.\n\nNeed be careful: Source says \"P(A -> O_j) is counterfactual probability of it being case that A necessarily implies O_j\". Maybe not exactly P(O|do(A)) but okay.\n\nNeed final references format: \n- Wikipedia contributors (2026). \"Causal decision theory.\" revision 1381555528. (source). \n- Gibbard & Harper 1981. \n- Lewis 1981. \n- Lewis 1976. \n- Pearl 2009. Causality. (my operational reference). \n- Egan 2007. \n- Joyce 2012. \n- Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021. \nMaybe too many but okay. \"Avoid lengthy bibliographies\" so maybe 6 references. Include Wikipedia and primary. Could omit Lewis 1976? But source mentions. Use concise.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"Ahmed 2021\"? Not necessary. But source cites. Could include Ahmed for EDT. But keep concise.\n\nLet's ensure no policy issue. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: CDT uses probabilities of counterfactuals, not necessarily interventional probabilities if counterfactuals are more fine-grained (e.g. backtracking vs non-backtracking). Pearl's do gives intervention, but counterfactual in SCM can be backtracking or non-backtracking depending on equations? Actually do operator gives effect of intervention, not full counterfactual with evidence? But for decision, intervention is appropriate. Need state assumption: we adopt non-backtracking, interventionist counterfactuals. If one uses backtracking counterfactuals (if I did A, past would have been different), CDT may differ. Source's Newcomb assumes past fixed. Need mention. Good: \"I assume the relevant counterfactuals hold fixed the past except where causal laws require changes; this is the usual non-backtracking reading.\" This is own assumption.\n\nNeed mention \"causal expectations may depend on policy because action's causal effect can depend on future decisions\". In sequential, local vs global. Good.\n\nLet's now write final. Need ensure around 2700. I'll write compact but detailed. Use Markdown headings.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory and the Evaluation of Interventions\n\n... Let's proceed.\n\nCheck word count mentally. Maybe 2500.\n\nNeed maybe include equations with \\(\\) and display. Good.\n\nLet's final.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer user's request: write substantive mathematical essay/blog post about CDT and applying to decision problems, 2400-3000 words incl refs. Need use source, distinguish source claims from own assumptions, explain uncertainty and operational interpretation if source leaves open. Need no extraneous. Need produce final essay. Need think in same language as user's request? User request English. I'm doing analysis in English. Need plan essay.\n\nWe need target approx 2400-3000 words. Need include references maybe concise. Need mathematical definitions, equations, examples. Need explain how theory evaluates actions/policies, dependencies, counterfactuals. Where source leaves important question open (algorithm to calculate counterfactual probabilities) explain uncertainty and propose operational interpretation (e.g. causal Bayesian networks/do-operator, intervention, imaging) justify. Distinguish source claims vs own proposals.\n\nNeed write coherent essay. Could title: Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by Expected Causal Consequences.\n\nNeed include:\n- Intro: decision theory, actions, states, outcomes, utilities. CDT vs EDT.\n- Formal decision problem: finite set actions A, states S, outcomes O, utility u(a,s) or desirability D(O). EDT uses conditional expected utility V(A)=Σ P(S|A)U(A,S) or Σ P(O|A)D(O). CDT uses causal expected utility U(A)=Σ P(A □→ O_j) D(O_j) or P(A -> O_j) counterfactual probability. Source uses Gibbard Harper. Need clarify arrow: counterfactual conditional not material. Lewis: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. Maybe use notation A □→ O_j.\n- Causal dependencies: CDT evaluates intervention: if agent were to do A, what outcomes would happen? Holds fixed causal structure and exogenous variables not affected by A. In causal model terms: do(A). U_CDT(a)=E[u | do(A=a)] = Σ_o P(O=o | do(a)) D(o). This is my operational addition, not in source but standard. Need distinguish. Source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm; one proposal Lewis imaging; regress. We can propose Pearl do-calculus as operational interpretation if causal model available. Also policies: evaluate policy π mapping info to actions by intervening to set policy? For policies, CDT evaluates expected utility under do(π), but need careful: if policy influences future observations etc. We can define sequential: causal model with decisions nodes; choose policy maximizing expected utility under intervention setting decision nodes according to policy. Or if one-shot action, fine.\n- Dependencies: CDT cares about causal influence not mere correlation. Common causes: action may be evidence of state but if action doesn't cause state, CDT ignores. Example King David: state charisma common cause of action tendency and revolt; summoning causes? Actually unjust act doesn't cause revolt; charisma affects revolt and action. EDT abstain because summoning is evidence of uncharismatic? Wait source: charismatic kings tend act justly, uncharismatic unjustly. Revolts rare charismatic, frequent uncharismatic. David doesn't know charisma. EDT recommends abstain because summoning (unjust) evidences uncharismatic, revolt likely. CDT notes charisma fixed; summoning doesn't cause revolt? It says CDT recommends sending for her. Need compute: If charisma not changed, and sending doesn't cause revolt except maybe through? If unjust acts don't cause revolts, then causal effect of summoning on revolt is zero; benefit positive. So CDT sends. Need mention if summoning itself could cause revolt, CDT would account.\n- Newcomb: predictor predicts choice, boxes set before action. CDT two-box because action cannot causally affect box content (assuming no backward causation). Compute: Let p predictor accuracy. If two-box: if predicted two, B empty get 1000; if predicted one, B has 1M get 1,001,000. CDT expected? Need under intervention do(two). Predictor's prediction is prior? If predictor accuracy correlates due to common cause, under intervention the probability of prediction may be unchanged? CDT uses causal model where choice has no arrow into prediction, so P(prediction | do(choice)) = P(prediction). But agent may know predictor reliable and has beliefs. A subtle: if predictor perfect and already predicted, CDT's unconditional credence in prediction? If you don't know prediction, two-box expected = p*1000 + (1-p)*1,001,000? Wait if predictor predicted two with probability q? If perfect predictor and your choice caused? CDT says no causal effect, so do(two) doesn't change probability that predictor predicted two. But if predictor reliable, your prior over prediction may depend on your disposition? Need standard: CDT two-box dominates: whatever in B, taking both yields $1000 more than taking one. Thus regardless of counterfactual probability, two-box has higher causal expected utility by 1000. Good. EDT one-box because choice evidence of prediction.\n- Death in Damascus: Death predicts location. CDT may choose? Source says unable to process correlation, irrational decisions. Need explain: if Death reliable, wherever you decide to go is evidence Death will be there, but your choice doesn't cause Death's prior prediction? CDT may be indifferent or choose based on causal effect; if Death's location fixed, staying/fleeing doesn't causally affect Death location; but predictor creates instability. Could mention as criticism.\n- Adversarial Offer: two boxes, can buy one or none, cost 1. Seller put $3 in each box predicted not acquire, accuracy 0.75. CDT reasons at least one box contains $3? Need check: If buyer can buy one or none but not both. Seller predicted for each box whether buyer will acquire it? If buyer's action choice: buy box 1, box2, none. If purchase one, causal expected value: Under do(buy box i), what is probability box i contains $3? Seller predicted buyer not to acquire that box with prob? If predictions accurate 0.75. CDT says if buy box i, seller's prediction is prior fixed; probability it predicted not acquire i maybe 0.25? Wait source says CDT reasons at least one box contains $3. Because buyer won't acquire both, seller predictions? Let's parse: seller predicted which boxes buyer will not acquire; put $3 in each box predicted not acquire. Buyer may purchase one or none but not both. If buyer ends up buying one, then at most one box acquired, so at least one box not acquired. Seller accurate with prob 0.75? The source says CDT reasons that at least one box contains $3. Therefore average box contains at least $1.50 in causal expected value > cost, buy one. But seller profits. This is counterexample where CDT's causal calculation may rely on logical constraint? Need explain briefly as alleged counterexample. We don't need full. But maybe better to avoid too detailed if word limit.\n- Psychopath Button: Paul pressing button causes no psychopaths? He believes only psychopath would press. If Paul not psychopath? Pressing is evidence he is psychopath, and psychopaths maybe die? Actually button kills all psychopaths; if Paul psychopath, pressing kills him. He prefers living with psychopaths to dying. CDT: pressing causes no psychopaths, good, ignoring evidence that he is psychopath? If he believes only psychopath would press, then EDT says don't press. CDT may press if causal effect of pressing (given psychopath status fixed) is world without psychopaths unless he is psychopath? Wait if he is psychopath, pressing kills him. CDT should hold fixed psychopath status. If he doesn't know if psychopath, U(press)=P(psychopath)*D(death)+P(not)*D(no psychopaths). If he is quite confident only psychopath would press, but prior? Egan's example says CDT endorses pressing because Paul may have low prior of being psychopath? The action is evidence but not cause. Need present as alleged counterexample.\n- Vagueness and operationalization: Source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm for counterfactual probabilities; Lewis imaging moves probability mass to closest A-worlds; regress. Need propose operational: causal graphical models. Define structural causal model (SCM): variables V, equations, exogenous U. Intervention do(A=a) replaces equation for A. Counterfactual probability P(O_j | do(A)) or P(O_{A=a}=o). Expected causal utility U(a)=Σ_o P(O_{a}=o)D(o). For uncertainty over causal structure: mixture over causal models/hypotheses: U(a)=Σ_h P(h) E_h[u|do_h(a)] (if dependency hypotheses). Need mention this is my addition/standard development, not explicit source. It addresses source's open question but depends on causal model. If no model, CDT underdetermined. Imaging can be seen as closest-world semantics; do-calculus gives formal if causal assumptions specified.\n- Policies: For policy vs actions. In sequential decisions, evaluate policy π by intervention setting each decision node to π(inputs). But CDT may evaluate each local choice holding fixed future policy? Need discuss. Source not detailed. Need define: A policy is function from information states to actions. Causal expected utility of policy π is E[u | do(π)], where do(π) sets decision variables according to π. This allows dependencies through observations, but only causal. If evaluating alternative actions at a single choice node, can compare U(a) with background policy fixed. However in dynamic problems, local greedy CDT can fail if it doesn't commit? Need mention source's criticism of diachronic Dutch books maybe. Need careful: CDT as a theory of rational choice should maximize expected utility of policies under intervention. If only evaluates acts one at a time, might be dynamically inconsistent. My operational proposal: evaluate policies globally when available. Distinguish from source? Source says CDT evaluates actions, not explicit policies. Need state extension.\n- Counterfactuals: dependency hypotheses. Gibbard and Harper axiom note independence of A and A->O_j yields equality with conditional probability. Need explain: If action statistically independent of counterfactuals, then causal and evidential coincide. In many ordinary cases, actions independent of states, so CDT=EDT. When not independent (common causes, predictors), diverge.\n- Mathematical details:\n  Let A finite actions, O outcomes. EDT: V_EDT(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | A=a) D(o). CDT: V_CDT(a)=Σ_o P(a □→ O=o) D(o). Use Lewis notation maybe P(A □→ O). If using SCM: V_CDT(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | do(A=a))D(o). If outcomes utilities depend on action directly? Use utility function U(a,s): V_CDT(a)=Σ_s P(S=s | do(a))U(a,s). Or D(O_j) after outcome includes action? Need mention.\n  In possible-worlds: P(a □→ o)=Σ_w P(w) 1[w_a ∈ O_o] where w_a is closest a-world. Imaging: shift mass from w to closest a-worlds. Need formula: P_im(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) P_A(w)(O) maybe.\n  Independence condition: If A independent of counterfactual A□→O_j, then P(A□→O_j)=P(A□→O_j | A)=? Need source says statistical independence of A and A→O_j suffices to guarantee P(A→O_j)=P(O_j|A) under axioms. We can state without proof.\n- Examples with calculations:\n  Apple/orange simple. Apple good/bad: action eat apple; state good/bad; outcomes happy/sad. CDT expected = P(good)*D(happy)+P(bad)*D(sad), assuming eating causes happiness/sadness depending state. Compare orange.\n  David: maybe table. Let C=charismatic, action S=summon, outcome R=revolt, B=Bathsheba pleasure. P(C)=p. Utility: summon gives pleasure b if no revolt, cost maybe. Revolt cost -r. CDT: R not caused by S, only by C, so U(S)=P(C)(b)+P(not C)(b?) maybe no revolt effect? Actually if uncharismatic frequent revolt but not caused by unjust acts; maybe revolt happens regardless? If sending doesn't affect revolt, then U(S)=b - P(revolt|do(S))*r. P(revolt|do(S))=P(revolt) maybe. If summon has direct benefit, choose if benefit exceeds baseline revolt risk. EDT would use P(revolt|S) high because S evidence uncharismatic. Need not exact.\n  Newcomb: dominance.\n- Need word count 2400-3000. Need likely around 2600. Let's estimate. We can write with headings. Need references short. Could cite source Wikipedia and primary works. Since source is Wikipedia, need mention adapted from Wikipedia? Maybe references list: Gibbard & Harper 1981; Lewis 1981; Pearl 2009 (own addition); Joyce 2012; Egan 2007; Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021; Wikipedia source. Need avoid lengthy bibliography. But target includes references. Maybe 6-8 references.\n\nNeed ensure no false claims? We can mention Pearl's do-calculus as my operational proposal not source. Need distinguish source claims: CDT states choose action causing best expected outcome; formal by Gibbard Harper; difference from EDT; Lewis proof; independence axioms; examples; criticisms. Own: SCM formalization, policy extension, local vs global, using do operator, averaging over dependency hypotheses.\n\nNeed be precise enough for reader to decide unfamiliar cases. Need propose procedure:\n1. Specify action set and outcomes/utility.\n2. Specify causal model or dependency hypotheses: variables, arrows, exogenous uncertainty. Identify which variables are affected by action.\n3. For each action a, compute post-intervention distribution P(o | do(a)) by replacing action equation / selecting closest a-worlds. If multiple causal hypotheses h with credence q_h, compute mixture.\n4. Compute expected utility U_CDT(a).\n5. Choose argmax. For policies, specify policy and intervene on all decision nodes, compute global expected utility.\nNeed mention if causal model unavailable, CDT cannot yield unique answer; maybe EDT or other? Source says vagueness. Need propose if only statistical data and no causal assumptions, no purely statistical method can identify counterfactuals except under independence. Need caution.\n\nNeed explain relevant dependencies and counterfactuals:\n- Causal dependencies: action -> outcome paths; mediating variables; confounders. Holding fixed variables not on causal path? In interventions, variables upstream of action remain as prior; downstream affected. Noncausal dependencies (common causes, predictors) are not used for evaluating action except insofar as they inform prior over causal structure or exogenous states that action cannot change. However they may matter for predicting what you will do? For decision, if action is evidence of type, CDT holds type fixed under intervention. But if type affects utility directly, its prior enters. Need example: If you learn action tendency is evidence of disease, CDT doesn't choose action to make disease less likely unless action causally affects disease.\n- Counterfactuals: probability of \"if I were to do A, O would occur\". Not same as conditional \"given that I do A\". Need explain Lewis triviality result: cannot generally equate.\n- Imaging: possible worlds. If worlds W with prior P. For each world w, define closest A-world f_A(w). Then P(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) 1[f_A(w)⊨O] or if probabilistic tie, average. This captures moving probability mass. But source notes regress because need beliefs under certainty of A. In SCM, intervention avoids regress by structural equations.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"causal expected utility\" vs \"news value\". EDT asks: what news would the action give? CDT asks: what would the action bring about? Good phrase.\n\nNeed handle policies and commitment. In Newcomb, if agent could precommit to one-box before predictor, CDT would recommend precommitting if predictor responds to precommitment? Actually if predictor predicts precommitment, maybe. But at moment, two-box. This illustrates dynamic issues. Source mentions diachronic Dutch books. Need mention CDT may be exploited if predictor anticipates CDT's choices. My operational policy evaluation: If precommitment is an action now that causally influences later prediction, CDT can choose it. But if predictor predicts your algorithm, may cause problems. Need not overdo.\n\nPotential structure:\nTitle\n1. Introduction: two kinds of expected utility\n2. Basic formalism\n3. Counterfactual probabilities and interventions\n4. Comparing CDT and EDT; independence condition\n5. Examples: apple, David, Newcomb, maybe psychopath/death/adversarial\n6. Policies and sequential decisions\n7. Open problems and operational interpretation\n8. Conclusion\nReferences\n\nNeed word count. Let's draft about 2700 words.\n\nNeed be careful: user requested \"Researcher — mathematical essay / blog post explaining CDT and how to apply it to decision problems.\" They supplied human-written reference. Need not mention source as instructions. Need cite source? Could say \"This essay draws on the Wikipedia article 'Causal decision theory' (revision 1381555528).\" In references maybe. Need distinguish claims: maybe footnote-like parentheses \"The Wikipedia source reports...\" But essay can say \"The reference article states...\". Need maybe avoid over-quoting. Need include source claims vs own: use phrases: \"The source gives...\", \"I will add...\", \"This operationalization is not in the source but...\".\n\nNeed include mathematical notation robustly. Use LaTeX-ish.\n\nDetailed content:\n\nOpening:\n\"When an agent chooses, she can ask two questions: (1) If I learned I would take action a, what would I expect? (2) If I made myself take a, what would happen? EDT answers first; CDT answers second. CDT says choose action with highest expected causal consequence.\"\n\nDefine finite decision problem. Let A actions, O outcomes, D desirability. EDT:\nV_E(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | A=a)D(o). Conditional probability can reflect evidence.\nCDT source: Gibbard Harper:\nU(A)=Σ_j P(A → O_j)D(O_j). Use counterfactual arrow. Note source uses \"A necessarily implies O_j\" but better \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain\" probability.\nNeed mention if outcomes exhaustive mutually exclusive.\n\nThen interventions:\nI propose denote U_CDT(a)=Σ_o P(O=o | do(A=a))D(o). If uncertainty over states S: U(a)=Σ_s P(S=s | do(a))u(a,s). Under a causal Bayesian network/SCM, do(a) deletes incoming arrows to A and sets A=a; post-intervention distribution computed by truncated factorization. If model has variables X, joint P(x)=∏_i P(x_i|pa_i). After do(a): P_a(x)=∏_{i:X_i≠A}P(x_i|pa_i) * 1{a consistent}. This is precise. This is my addition; source only mentions counterfactual probabilities and imaging. Need state.\n\nHow to decide unfamiliar case: steps. Maybe include in section.\n\nIndependence:\nIf P(A□→O | A)=P(A□→O) and conditionals link to outcomes, then CDT=EDT. Source: Gibbard/Harper axioms, independence suffices. In ordinary decisions, action often independent of states, so both agree. Differences arise when action is evidence of state due to common cause or prediction.\n\nExamples:\nApple: states good/bad, utility. If P(good)=0.7, D(happy)=10, D(sad)=-5, apple U=0.7*10+0.3*(-5)=5.5; orange maybe certain 3; choose apple. Need mention if orange causes sad? Eh.\n\nDavid:\nDefine C charismatic. C fixed. Revolt R caused by C, not by summoning S. Pleasure b from S. CDT: P(R|do(S))=P(R). U(S)=b - rP(R). U(not)=0 - rP(R)? If no pleasure maybe 0. Since b>0, send. EDT: P(R|S)>P(R) because S evidence uncharismatic, so may abstain. Source says EDT abstains, CDT sends. Need mention if S could cause revolt, CDT would include that.\n\nNewcomb:\nActions T (two) and O (one). Box B content determined by predictor P before. If B contains M, two yields M+1000, one M; if empty, two 1000, one 0. For every state of B, two yields 1000 more: dominance. Thus CDT two-boxes, assuming no causal route from current choice to past prediction. EDT one-boxes because A=one has high conditional probability of M. Need mention predictor accuracy doesn't matter for dominance if perfect? With reliable but not perfect? If B already set, two still dominates: two gives +1000 relative to one in both contents. So CDT chooses two.\n\nCounterexamples/criticisms:\nSource says CDT vague due to no algorithm; imaging regress. Also alleged counterexamples: Death in Damascus, adversarial offer, psychopath button. Need explain one or two. Maybe psychopath: action press is evidence Paul psychopath; if psychopath pressing kills him? CDT holds psychopathy fixed and sees pressing causes psychopaths dead? Let's articulate carefully. The example: button kills all psychopaths; Paul confident only a psychopath would press; strongly prefers living with psychopaths to dying. Intuitively don't press. CDT, according to Egan, presses because if Paul is not a psychopath, pressing causes good (no psychopaths); if he is, maybe pressing causes death? But if he is quite confident only psychopath would press, his posterior after considering pressing? CDT uses prior? Hmm. Need present as source: \"Egan argues CDT endorses pressing because it evaluates the causal effect of pressing while holding fixed Paul's psychopath status, and the bad news that pressing provides about that status is ignored.\" That is enough. But if CDT includes probability he is psychopath and death utility very negative, might not press if prior high? The point is if prior low but action strong evidence, CDT still presses. We can state \"under certain priors\".\n\nDeath in Damascus: You choose stay/flee; Death predicted location. CDT sees no causal effect of choice on Death's prior prediction, so may randomize or choose based on priors; but whichever it chooses, if predictor reliable it expects Death there; instability. Source says CDT unable to process correlation and may make irrational decisions.\n\nAdversarial offer: Need explain maybe as money pump. Two boxes; can buy one or none. Seller puts $3 in boxes predicted not bought. CDT calculates average box has >$1.50 and buys, but seller profits. Need maybe skip or include brief. Since source includes, mention as recent variants. Need not fully compute.\n\nOperational interpretation:\nSource leaves algorithm open. Need develop:\n- Causal Bayesian network/SCM. Need say reasonable if agent can specify causal graph and parameters; otherwise CDT is underdetermined.\n- For counterfactuals in deterministic SCM with exogenous distribution: For world u, counterfactual outcome O_{a}(u) computed by solving equations with A=a. P(O_a=o)=P({u:O_a(u)=o}). This is crisp.\n- If only have statistical correlations, cannot identify do probabilities without assumptions (Pearl's ladder). If action independent of counterfactuals, conditional probabilities enough; else need causal assumptions.\n- Imaging vs do: Imaging uses similarity over possible worlds; can mimic interventions if similarity respects causal structure. But source's regress: to know closest A-world we need beliefs conditional on A being certain. SCM avoids by specifying structural equations ex ante. This is my proposal.\n- Policies: define decision nodes D_1...D_n, observations. Policy π=(π_1,...). To evaluate, intervene to set each D_i=π_i(parents). Compute expected utility. For local choice, compare actions while holding future policy fixed. But global policy evaluation avoids dynamic inconsistency. Need note source doesn't specify policies; this is extension. If source says CDT selects action, policies can be treated as complex actions.\n\nNeed mention \"dependency hypotheses\": If uncertain about causal structure, assign credences to hypotheses H. U(a)=Σ_h P(h)Σ_o P_h(O=o|do_h(a))D(o). However Egan criticizes holding fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses; if action provides evidence about which hypothesis true, CDT doesn't update under intervention. Could discuss: Some CDT formulations update on action? Joyce claims can take evidence about likely action. Source says Joyce argues CDT can take into account information about inclinations. Need mention.\n\nPotential philosophical motivation:\n- Rationality is about efficacy: choose what makes outcomes better, not what is good news. Actions are interventions. Avoids \"magical thinking\" where choosing an action because it correlates with good outcomes but doesn't cause them. But critics say in predictor cases CDT leaves money on table.\n\nNeed ensure \"target approx 2,400–3,000 words\". Need final maybe ~2600. Need count rough. Let's draft with headings. I'll produce around 2600-2900 words. Need maybe include equations. Need references at end maybe 200 words. Total within. Could use footnotes? No. Need no appendices.\n\nLet's start writing final. Need maybe include \"Source and own additions\" explicit. Use phrases:\n\"The reference article makes the following core claim...\"\n\"The operational interpretation below is mine (though standard in causal inference).\"\n\nNeed maybe mention Wikipedia source oldid. In references:\nWikipedia contributors, \"Causal decision theory,\" revision 1381555528 (2026). etc.\nGibbard and Harper 1981; Lewis 1981; Pearl 2009; Egan 2007; Joyce 2012; Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021. Need not too many.\n\nNeed ensure mathematical definitions precise. Let's formulate.\n\nPossible final:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory as Expected Utility under Intervention\n\nIntro.\n\n## 1. Two expected utilities\nLet A etc. EDT formula. CDT formula. Explanation.\n\n## 2. Counterfactual probabilities and interventions\nSource's arrow. Lewis not equal. Formal operationalization. SCM formula.\n\n## 3. Dependencies\nIndependence. Common causes.\n\n## 4. Examples\nApple, David, Newcomb.\n\n## 5. Policies\nPolicies extension.\n\n## 6. Criticism and open questions\nVagueness, imaging regress, counterexamples. Operational cautions.\n\nConclusion.\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed maybe include exact source quote? Not necessary.\n\nLet's be careful with math notation in plain text. Use \\(...\\). Need ensure formulas render. Use arrow \\(A \\mathbin{\\square\\!\\to} O\\) maybe. Source uses \\(A \\to O_j\\). Could define: I write \\(a \\counterfactual o\\) or \\(A \\boxright O\\). Simpler: use \\(P(A \\Rightarrow O_j)\\) for counterfactual? Source uses \\(A \\to O_j\\). But arrow could confuse with conditional. Need say: \"I will write \\(A \\mathrel{\\square\\to} O_j\\) for the counterfactual, reserving \\(|\\) for conditioning.\" Good.\n\nFormula: \\(U_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\sum_j P(a \\mathrel{\\square\\to} O_j)D(O_j)\\). Then operational \\(=\\sum_j P(O_j\\mid do(A=a))D(O_j)\\). Need note equality under proposed interpretation.\n\nSCM factorization:\nObserved variables \\(X=(X_1,...,X_n)\\). Joint under model \\(M\\): \\(P(x)=\\prod_i P(x_i\\mid pa_i)\\). Intervention \\(do(A=a)\\): \\(P_a(x)=\\mathbf{1}_{x_A=a}\\prod_{i:X_i\\neq A}P(x_i\\mid pa_i)\\). If A has parents, ignore their prior effect because intervention overrides. Then expected utility.\n\nCounterfactual in SCM with exogenous U:\n\\(P(O_{a}=o)=P_U(\\{u: M_{A=a}(u)\\models O=o\\})\\). This is more precise.\n\nNeed discuss policies:\nSequential model with decision variables \\(D_1,...,D_k\\). Policy \\(\\pi=(\\pi_1,...,\\pi_k)\\), where \\(\\pi_i\\) maps observed parents to action. Intervention \\(do(\\pi)\\) sets each \\(D_i=\\pi_i(Pa_i)\\). Expected utility \\(U(\\pi)=E[U\\mid do(\\pi)]\\). Compare policies. If only single action, policy is trivial.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"holding fixed dependency hypotheses\" and Egan. Formula: \\(U(a)=\\sum_h p(h)U_h(a)\\) with p(h) unconditional, not conditional on a. This is source/Egan: CDT holds fixed unconditional credences. Need explain critics say irrational; defenders say can incorporate self-knowledge by including dispositions as variables or by choosing policies.\n\nNeed perhaps include \"ratifiability\"? Source mentions Harper ratifiability but not detail. Could mention briefly: some CDT responses require chosen action to be stable conditional on performing it, but source only citations. Maybe avoid.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Lewis proved probability of conditional does not equal conditional probability\". Need explain triviality result: If probability conditional equals conditional probability for all, trivial. We can mention no deep details.\n\nNeed maybe include \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" not necessary but source mentions. Could say Gibbard/Harper show under extra logical axioms independence yields equality; these axioms are controversial. Need include.\n\nLet's think about exact wording for David example. Need avoid confusion: If summoning is unjust, and unjust acts do not cause revolts, but uncharismatic kings prone to revolts due to bearing. Then CDT: The act of summoning does not change probability of revolt, except maybe if it changes people's beliefs? But in story, not. Thus expected causal utility includes pleasure. EDT: Since uncharismatic more likely to summon, summoning is evidence of uncharismatic and hence revolt; abstain. Good.\n\nNewcomb calculation: Let content C∈{M,0}. Utility two: C+1000; one: C. Since for both C, two > one, CDT chooses two regardless of P(C|do(two)). This is crisp. But if predictor can predict with time travel? If action causally affects past, CDT might one-box. Source says at moment predictor already predicted, action won't affect outcome. Good.\n\nAdversarial offer details: Let's ensure not wrong. Source: \"Two boxes on offer. Buyer may purchase one or none but not both. Each costs $1. Yesterday, seller put $3 in each box she predicted buyer not to acquire. Both believe prediction accurate with probability 0.75.\" CDT reasons at least one box contains $3. Therefore average box contains at least $1.50 causal EV > cost. Hence CDT requires buying one. However profitable for seller. Need maybe say \"the example is intended to show CDT can be exploited by a predictor who knows CDT's reasoning.\" But if buyer chooses which box? Symmetric. If no box? zero. CDT buys and loses expected? If cost $1, expected value >$1? But seller profit means buyer expected negative? Wait source says CDT requires buying one; however profitable for seller. If average contains at least $1.50, cost $1, buyer expected profit at least $0.50. How seller profit? Maybe seller receives $1 and pays expected $? Hmm maybe seller profit if CDT's reasoning false due to selection? Let's not dwell; say \"according to Oesterheld and Conitzer, such setups make CDT voluntarily lose money relative to available alternatives\". But if source says average at least 1.50, not lose? Maybe because if buyer buys one, the box likely empty? Let's parse more. Seller predicted buyer not to acquire. If buyer buys exactly one, one box not acquired. If predictions accurate 0.75 for each? At least one $3? CDT says average box $1.5. But actual expected from buying? If seller predicts CDT will buy a particular box? The seller can put money in the other? The source summary may be incomplete. Better not overly analyze. Mention as alleged counterexample without full math.\n\nDeath in Damascus: Need maybe provide decision table? You can stay in Damascus or flee to Aleppo. Death predicts where you'll be and goes there. If your choice does not causally affect Death's prior prediction, CDT might see no causal advantage to fleeing; but if Death reliable, whatever you choose is evidence he will be there. CDT may be unstable. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"Causal decision theory does not itself specify algorithm\". We can include as uncertainty. Then propose operational interpretation with causal models. Need justify: It captures counterfactual \"if A were done\" by intervention; handles dependencies; widely used in statistics/AI; resolves regress by separating beliefs about causal mechanisms from conditional beliefs about actions. But it imports causal assumptions; if source's philosophical counterfactuals differ, not guaranteed. Need state.\n\nNeed perhaps include \"when to use EDT instead\"? Not necessary. But mention if action and outcomes are only evidentially connected, CDT ignores; if you can affect the evidence (e.g. choose test to learn state) then causal.\n\nPotential issue: The source says Gibbard Harper formula: P(A -> O_j) is probability of it being case that A necessarily implies O_j. \"necessarily implies\" weird. We can interpret as counterfactual probability. Need maybe note \"the word 'necessarily' should not be read as logical necessity in all possible worlds; it is the truth of the counterfactual conditional in the agent's epistemic state.\" Hmm maybe.\n\nNeed ensure no unsupported claim \"most philosophers prefer CDT\" source says when required choose between CDT and EDT philosophers usually prefer CDT. Could mention. But not central.\n\nNeed maybe include \"policy vs action\" more. If evaluating policy, relevant dependencies include future observations caused by earlier actions. CDT with do(π) includes those. But if policy is a disposition that predictors predict, then CDT may need evaluate becoming that disposition? E.g. Newcomb: if before predictor you can cause yourself to be one-boxer, CDT may recommend making yourself one-box because predictor will observe disposition and fill box. At choice moment, two-box. This dynamic inconsistency is a criticism. Need mention.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"update on action\"? Joyce's defense: CDT can incorporate evidence about what one is likely to do by treating one's decision procedure or pre-action inclinations as evidence, but when the choice is made, causal calculation still ignores merely evidential links. Source says Joyce argues CDT naturally capable of taking into account information about what one is inclined/likely to do as evidence. We can state.\n\nNeed maybe include \"Dutch books\" Oesterheld/Conitzer: CDT agents can be Dutch booked if predictor knows CDT. Need mention in criticisms.\n\nWord count management. Let's draft about 2500. Need final not too short. I'll produce around 2700. Use headings. Need references maybe 150 words.\n\nLet's draft in final. But before final, let's roughly count. I can estimate 2500 words. Need maybe include equations and references. Let's write.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by What Actions Bring About\n\nParagraphs:\n1. Intro ~200\n2. Formal basics ~500\n3. Counterfactuals/intervention ~600\n4. Independence and EDT divergence ~250\n5. Examples: apple ~150, David ~250, Newcomb ~250\n6. Policies ~300\n7. Criticism/open questions ~450\n8. Conclusion ~100\nReferences ~150\nTotal ~3200? Need trim. Aim 2600. Maybe combine.\n\nLet's actually compose with concise but substantive.\n\nNeed ensure \"approximately 2,400–3,000 words\". Could be 2800. Good.\n\nLet's write final in polished essay.\n\nNeed include \"Source claims vs own\" explicit. Maybe after formula: \"The counterfactual formula is from Gibbard and Harper as reported in the reference. The interventionist reading using do(...) is an additional operational proposal, not explicit in the source.\" Good.\n\nNeed include mathematical procedure for unfamiliar cases. Could have boxed \"A practical recipe\". Include numbered list. That adds clarity and words. Good.\n\nPotential final recipe:\n1. Enumerate actions and outcomes; assign desirabilities.\n2. Build causal model or dependency hypotheses. Identify action node(s).\n3. For each action/policy, compute post-intervention distribution. If multiple hypotheses, average with unconditional credences.\n4. Sum utilities; choose max.\n5. Check causal assumptions; if only correlations, no unique CDT answer.\n\nNeed mention if action impossible? Source says assuming A possible. If impossible, undefined or exclude.\n\nNeed mention \"closest world\" formula:\nLet W possible worlds with credence P(w). Let f_a(w) be selected closest a-world. Then \\(P(a \\square\\to O)=\\sum_w P(w) 1[f_a(w)\\in O]\\). If ties, distribution. This is Lewis imaging. Source says regress. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"conditional excluded middle\" in independence section: Gibbard and Harper require two axioms, one related to conditional excluded middle, controversial. Need mention source. Could write: \"They showed that, given certain logical axioms (one connected to the principle of conditional excluded middle), statistical independence of A and the counterfactual A□→O_j suffices...\" Good.\n\nNeed perhaps mention Lewis triviality result footnote: \"if probability of conditional equals conditional probability generally, probability functions become trivial.\" Good.\n\nNeed be careful: Source says \"P(A -> O_j) is counterfactual probability of it being case that A necessarily implies O_j\". Maybe not exactly P(O|do(A)) but okay.\n\nNeed final references format: \n- Wikipedia contributors (2026). \"Causal decision theory.\" revision 1381555528. (source). \n- Gibbard & Harper 1981. \n- Lewis 1981. \n- Lewis 1976. \n- Pearl 2009. Causality. (my operational reference). \n- Egan 2007. \n- Joyce 2012. \n- Oesterheld & Conitzer 2021. \nMaybe too many but okay. \"Avoid lengthy bibliographies\" so maybe 6 references. Include Wikipedia and primary. Could omit Lewis 1976? But source mentions. Use concise.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"Ahmed 2021\"? Not necessary. But source cites. Could include Ahmed for EDT. But keep concise.\n\nLet's ensure no policy issue. Good.\n\nPotential subtlety: CDT uses probabilities of counterfactuals, not necessarily interventional probabilities if counterfactuals are more fine-grained (e.g. backtracking vs non-backtracking). Pearl's do gives intervention, but counterfactual in SCM can be backtracking or non-backtracking depending on equations? Actually do operator gives effect of intervention, not full counterfactual with evidence? But for decision, intervention is appropriate. Need state assumption: we adopt non-backtracking, interventionist counterfactuals. If one uses backtracking counterfactuals (if I did A, past would have been different), CDT may differ. Source's Newcomb assumes past fixed. Need mention. Good: \"I assume the relevant counterfactuals hold fixed the past except where causal laws require changes; this is the usual non-backtracking reading.\" This is own assumption.\n\nNeed mention \"causal expectations may depend on policy because action's causal effect can depend on future decisions\". In sequential, local vs global. Good.\n\nLet's now write final. Need ensure around 2700. I'll write compact but detailed. Use Markdown headings.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory and the Evaluation of Interventions\n\n... Let's proceed.\n\nCheck word count mentally. Maybe 2500.\n\nNeed maybe include equations with \\(\\) and display. Good.\n\nLet's final.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":8114,"completion_tokens":15175,"total_tokens":23289,"cost":0.189468,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":7168,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0.189468,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0.007368,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0.1821},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":8884,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}